<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palden Thondup Namgyal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palden Thondup Namgyal was the twelfth and last Chogyal of Sikkim, reigning from 1963. His wish for more independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while discontent grew among the Nepali-speaking majority, led to unrest in the early 1970s. In 1975 Indian troops disarmed his guard, a referendum abolished the monarchy and Sikkim became a state of India. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palden-thondup-namgyal.jpg" alt="Palden Thondup Namgyal" width="320" height="532" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alice S. Kandell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palden_Thondup_Namgyal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Palden Thondup Namgyal was born on 23 May 1923 at the Royal Palace, Park Ridge, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangtok">Gangtok</a>. From the age of eight to eleven he studied under his uncle, Rimpoche Lhatsun, to be ordained a Buddhist monk, and he was recognised as the reincarnated leader of both the Phodong and Rumtek monasteries. He graduated from Bishop Cotton School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> in 1941. He hoped to study science at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, but his elder brother, the crown prince, an officer in the Indian Air Force, was killed in a plane crash in 1941, and Palden trained instead for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a> at Dehradun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He served as adviser for internal affairs to his father, Sir Tashi Namgyal, the 11th Chogyal, and led the negotiating team that established Sikkim's relationship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1949. In 1963 he married Hope Cooke, a 22-year-old American graduate of Sarah Lawrence College, and the marriage brought worldwide media attention to Sikkim. His father died soon afterwards and he was crowned Chogyal on an astrologically favourable date.</p><p>In 1975 the Prime Minister of Sikkim appealed to the Indian Parliament for Sikkim to become a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In April the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangtok">Gangtok</a> and disarmed the palace guards. He had financed Satyajit Ray's documentary Sikkim (1971) and was an amateur radio operator with the call-sign AC3PT. A referendum on 14 April 1975 voted 59,637 to 1,496 for Indian statehood and the removal of the Chogyal, who was under Indian army guard in his palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Namgyal and Hope Cooke divorced in 1980. He died of cancer at the Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center in New York City on 29 January 1982, aged 58, and 31 members of the State Legislative Assembly offered khadas in respect. His elder son, the former crown prince Tenzing Kunzang Jigme Namgyal, died in a car accident in 1978. His second son, Tobgyal Wangchuk Tenzing Namgyal, was named the 13th Chogyal, though the position no longer confers any official authority. His honours included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Padma Vibhushan in 1954 and the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palden Thondup Namgyal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Agitation against <b>Palden Thondup Namgyal</b> in the 1970s led to a 1975 referendum that abolished the monarchy, and Sikkim became India's 22nd state.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sikkim">Kingdom of Sikkim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangtok">Gangtok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.33,88.62&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Gangtok on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6879 1161 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Palden Thondup Namgyal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7329 1431h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7329 1431h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.33,88.62&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
